This is simple demonstration to set up a Valkey cluster and get it running in Kubernetes (minikube)

Steps to run the cluster :
1. minikube start --memory=4096 --cpus=4
2. kubectl apply -f valkey-service.yaml
3. kubectl apply -f valkey-statefulset.yaml

Wait for pods to spin-up. Then run,
kubectl get pods -l app=valkey

This will return the set of pods running (6 as oer the configuration)

To create a Valkey cluster :
kubectl exec -it valkey-0 -- sh
(where valkey-0 is the name of a pod. Feel free to select any pod to be master)

Inside the pod run :
valkey-cli --cluster create \
valkey-0.valkey:6379 \
valkey-1.valkey:6379 \
valkey-2.valkey:6379 \
valkey-3.valkey:6379 \
valkey-4.valkey:6379 \
valkey-5.valkey:6379 \
--cluster-replicas 1

✅ Cluster is now created.

Verify cluster :
kubectl exec -it valkey-0 -- valkey-cli cluster info
kubectl exec -it valkey-0 -- valkey-cli cluster nodes

(This should show 3 master nodes and 3 slave nodes)

View the cluster in minikube dashboard using :
minikube dashboard

Deleting Everything :
kubectl delete statefulset valkey
kubectl delete svc valkey